#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#     "httpx>=0.27",
# ]
# ///
"""coolify — Coolify CLI over the REST API. Drives a self-hosted Coolify
instance: list projects, resources, servers, applications, services and
databases; read their status and an application's logs; and deploy, restart,
stop or start them. Agent-invoked — run `coolify help` to reload this contract.

USAGE
  coolify <command> [options]

Identity-free: it operates on whatever team the resolved COOLIFY_TOKEN
authorizes, against the instance at COOLIFY_BASE_URL. Every resource is
addressed by its Coolify `uuid` (never a name or slug); a project's
environments come back inside the project object. Structured reads emit JSON
to stdout.

CONNECTIONS
  Configuration requires an explicit connections registry, even for one
  connection. Its values resolve from the cascade (first non-empty wins):
    1. flag           --base-url / --token
    2. project env    .env.local then .env, walking up from $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR (else cwd)
    3. user config    $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/coolify/credentials.env (default ~/.config/coolify/)
    4. process env    COOLIFY_BASE_URL / COOLIFY_TOKEN
  A secret never has a flag of its own that puts it on argv; --token exists for
  parity but prefer config/env. COOLIFY_BASE_URL is the instance root WITHOUT
  the /api/v1 suffix (e.g. https://coolify.example.com); the CLI appends it.

  More than one instance or team is declared in
  <project>/capabilities/coolify/connections.json:
    {
      "default": "<id>",
      "connections": {
        "<id>": { "base_url": "https://coolify.example.com",
                  "secret_env": "COOLIFY_<ID>_TOKEN",
                  "allow_write": true }
      }
    }
  The registry resolves project envelope first, else the machine-level
  ~/.config/coolify/connections.json; first found wins, never merged. The entry
  names the env var holding its token ("secret_env"); the value resolves through
  cascade tiers 2-4 above. "base_url" sits literally in the entry.

  `coolify connect` (below) writes one entry and its token in one act.

  Selection: --connection <id> (accepted anywhere on the command line).
  Unflagged, the registry's "default" pointer applies, else the sole entry;
  several entries without a pointer is refused as ambiguous.

  Inside a project, a connection declared in ~/.config/coolify/ is usable once
  the project grants it. Outside any project, connections, doctor and servers
  are machine reads: they use the machine's own connections read-only, unless
  a grant switched one off; every other verb that needs a connection is
  refused there.

  A Coolify API token is team-scoped and carries explicit abilities (read,
  read:sensitive, write, deploy, root). A connection without "allow_write": true
  resolves writability from WRITE_DEFAULT (true — deploys stay inside an instance
  the consumer owns); a connection set to false refuses deploy/start/stop/restart
  with exit 4.

COMMANDS
  connections
      Where every connection's values resolve from (tier, source, secrets
      masked). Local only — no network.

  connect <name> --url <url> (--token-stdin | --token-file <path> |
                              --token-env <KEY>)
          [--global | --project] [--default] [--ssh-key <path>]
      Pair an instance: write the connection entry and its token in one act,
      then run the doctor probe on it. The entry holds only non-secret values:
      "base_url", "secret_env" (COOLIFY_<NAME>_TOKEN, the key holding the
      token) and, with --ssh-key, "ssh": {"user": "root", "key_path": <path>}
      for reaching the instance's servers. The token is written to the
      credentials file of the same scope, mode 0600, single-quoted so the
      parser reads it back whole: --project (the default) writes the
      project's capabilities/coolify/connections.json and the project's
      .env.local; --global writes ~/.config/coolify/connections.json and
      ~/.config/coolify/credentials.env. Other entries and other lines are
      kept; an existing entry of the same name is replaced, its grant fields
      kept. --default points the scope's default at it. --url is the instance
      root, http(s)://host[:port], without /api/v1. --token-stdin reads the
      token from standard input (no echo on a terminal), --token-file from a
      file, --token-env resolves an env key through the cascade above. The
      token is never printed. Answers the entry's paths and the probe's
      result (version, team, abilities); a failed probe keeps the entry and
      the token and exits with the probe's category (2 or 5), so a fix needs
      `coolify --connection <name> doctor`, not a second pairing. A global
      connection used inside a project still needs that project's grant; the
      answer says so with the command. Outside any project the answer says
      "usable_here": true: the machine reads use it there, read-only.
      Repeating it is safe: it rewrites the same entry and the same line.

  doctor [--connection <id>]
      Validate token + reachability (GET /version, GET /teams/current), report
      the Coolify version, token team, and effective token abilities — every
      connection, or one. Ability checks are non-mutating; read:sensitive can
      be unknown when the team has no secret-bearing resource to inspect.
      Exit 0 all healthy / 2 missing-or-rejected token / 5 unreachable.

  projects [<uuid>]
      List projects, or get one project (its environments come inside it).
  projects create <name> [--description <text>]
      Create a new project. Returns the project_uuid and default environment.
      `projects create --name <name>` still works for one release, with a
      warning on stderr. Write verb - requires allow_write.
  resources
      Flat list of every deployable resource (uuid, name, type, status) —
      the lookup an agent runs to find the uuid to act on.
  sources
      List GitHub Apps and private/deploy keys with the UUIDs accepted by
      `app create --github-app` and `--private-deploy-key`. Secret key
      material is never emitted.
  applications [<uuid>]            List applications, or get one (full detail).
  services [<uuid>]               List services, or get one.
  databases [<uuid>]              List databases, or get one.
  servers [<uuid>]                List servers, or get one.
  deployments [<uuid>]            List active/queued deployments, or get one
                                  deployment by its uuid.
  logs <uuid> [--lines N]         An application's runtime logs (default 100).

  app create --project <uuid> --server <uuid> --environment <name>
      Create an application. Source-selecting flags (mutually exclusive):
      --git-repository <url> --git-branch <branch>  (public git)
          [--build-pack nixpacks|railpack|static|dockerfile|dockercompose]
          [--docker-compose-location <path>] [--dockerfile-location <path>]
      --private-deploy-key <uuid> (+ git flags above for private git with key)
      --github-app <uuid> (+ git flags above for private git with GitHub app)
      --image <registry/name:tag>  (registry image)
      --dockerfile <file|->  (inline dockerfile, raw content)
      Common optional: --name, --domains, --ports-exposes, --instant-deploy,
      --destination <uuid>, --base-directory, --watch-paths, and the
      health-check flags accepted by `app update`. Returns the application uuid.
      Write verb - requires allow_write.

      --instant-deploy creates without queuing, then deploys once, returning
      deployment_uuid and deployments alongside the new application's uuid.
      If deployment fails, the created application remains for inspection.

  app update <uuid> [options]
      Patch only the supplied application fields:
      --name --domains --ports-exposes --base-directory
      --dockerfile-location --docker-compose-location --build-pack
      --git-repository --git-branch --watch-paths
      --auto-deploy-enabled / --no-auto-deploy-enabled
      --health-check-enabled / --no-health-check-enabled
      --health-check-path --health-check-port --health-check-host
      --health-check-scheme --health-check-return-code
      --health-check-interval --health-check-timeout --health-check-retries
      --health-check-start-period
      --watch-paths accepts newline-separated glob patterns that filter which
      repository changes trigger a webhook deploy (e.g. "backend/**"). Pass an
      empty string to clear the field (deploy on all changes).
      Write verb - requires allow_write.

  service create --compose <file|-> --project <uuid> --server <uuid>
                 --environment <name> [--name <name>] [--instant-deploy]
                 [--destination <uuid>]
      Create a service from a docker-compose file. The compose file is
      base64-encoded before sending. Returns the service uuid.
      Write verb - requires allow_write.

  database create --engine <engine> --project <uuid> --server <uuid>
                  (--environment <name> | --environment-uuid <uuid>)
      Create a standalone database. --engine picks the endpoint
      (POST /databases/<engine>) and is one of:
        postgresql  mysql  mariadb  mongodb  redis  keydb  dragonfly  clickhouse
      Coolify accepts an environment by name or by uuid; supply at least one.
      Named optional flags: --name, --description, --image, --instant-deploy,
      --destination <uuid>.
      Every other body field is a pass-through, applied after the named flags:
        --set KEY=VALUE       sends VALUE as a JSON string
        --set-json KEY=JSON   sends a parsed JSON value (boolean, number, array)
      Both repeat. A secret field takes its value off the command line:
        --set-stdin KEY       reads VALUE from standard input (once)
        --set-file KEY=PATH   reads VALUE from a file
        --set-env KEY=ENV_KEY reads VALUE from an env key (cascade above)
      each sent as a JSON string. --set or --set-json on a password field
      (any name containing "password") still works for one release, with a
      warning on stderr. The capability sends these names and values as given
      and validates neither against the engine — Coolify answers an unknown or
      malformed field with 422 naming it. The engine credentials and tuning live
      here and differ per engine (postgres_user/postgres_password/postgres_db,
      mysql_root_password, mariadb_database, mongo_initdb_root_username,
      redis_password, keydb_password, dragonfly_password, clickhouse_admin_user
      and their neighbours), next to the fields every engine shares (is_public,
      public_port, tags, the limits_* set). Coolify wants each *_conf field
      base64-encoded, and silently drops is_public when public_port is absent.
      Returns the database uuid, with internal_db_url on every engine except
      dragonfly, and external_db_url only on a public database, their
      passwords redacted (see SECRETS); --reveal prints them to a terminal.
      Write verb - requires allow_write.

  app|service|database delete <uuid> --yes [--delete-volumes]
      [--delete-configurations] [--docker-cleanup] [--delete-connected-networks]
      Delete a resource. Cleanup options default to false, including volumes;
      each option explicitly opts into that cleanup. Requires allow_write.
  projects delete <uuid> --yes
      Delete an empty project. Requires allow_write; remove its resources first.

  deploy [<uuid>] [--force] [--tag T]
      Trigger a deployment (POST /deploy). Returns deployments with resource_uuid
      and deployment_uuid (applications; Coolify returns no deployment uuid for
      services or databases). --force rebuilds without cache; --tag deploys
      every resource with a tag, rather than selecting an image tag.
  wait <uuid> [--deployment <uuid>] [--timeout <s>]
      Poll until the resource reports running:healthy and, when specified, the
      deployment reports finished. Default timeout: 600 seconds, poll: 5 seconds.
      Starting/exited states are retried. Failed/cancelled deployments exit 5;
      timeout exits 5 wait_timeout with the last resource and deployment states.
      Reads only. A missing resource exits 3.
  start <uuid>   --type application|service|database
  stop <uuid>    --type application|service|database
  restart <uuid> --type application|service|database
      In-place lifecycle on a running resource. --type selects the endpoint
      family (defaults to application).

  env list <uuid> [--type application|service]
      List environment variables for an application or service. Every row has
      `value_hidden`; true means Coolify redacted the value (normally because
      the token lacks effective read:sensitive access), not that it is empty.
  env set <uuid> <KEY> [--value-stdin | --value-file <path> |
                        --value-env <ENV_KEY>] [--type application|service]
      Set or update an environment variable. The value comes from standard
      input (the default, no echo on a terminal, one trailing newline
      dropped), a file, or an env key resolved through the cascade above.
      `env set <uuid> <KEY> <VALUE>` still works for one release, with a
      warning on stderr. Write verb - requires allow_write.
  env rm <uuid> <KEY> [--type application|service]
      Remove an environment variable. Write verb - requires allow_write.
  env bulk <uuid> [--type application|service]
      Bulk update environment variables (PATCH). Reads KEY=VALUE pairs from
      stdin, one per line. Write verb - requires allow_write.

SECRETS
  A secret never goes on the command line: every verb that takes one reads it
  from standard input, a file or an env key. Nothing prints one: every answer
  redacts the password in a connection URL (scheme://user:<redacted>@host) and
  the value of every field whose name contains "password". --reveal, on the
  reads (projects, resources, applications, services, databases, servers,
  deployments, logs, env list) and on database create, prints them in full,
  and is refused with exit 4 unless stdout is a terminal.

Contract commands (the same on every capability):
  coolify stub                                 One-line awareness text.
  coolify manifest --json                      Machine-readable declaration.
  coolify guide [bootstrap|headless-apps]      Guide topics / one guide body.
  coolify ids list|get <l>|set <l> <v>|rm <l>  The project identifiers envelope
              [--note <text>]                   (capabilities/coolify/identifiers.json).
  coolify refs                                 Menu of the project's reference files.
  coolify inventory [--network]                What this capability holds in
                                               this project (--network adds
                                               remote detail).

EXIT CODES
  0 success · 2 auth/credentials (401/403, missing token) · 3 not found (404) ·
  4 policy refusal — the project gate (capabilities/settings.json) on any
  verb, a write verb on a read-only connection, or --reveal without a
  terminal; ask the user, never lift a gate yourself · 5 server/network (timeout, 429 after retries, 5xx) ·
  6 input (a malformed argument, an unknown/ambiguous connection)
"""

from __future__ import annotations

